  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nThe general setup indicates a warm frontal boundary moving into the \nregion and then stalling somewhere over CNY and NEPA for the first \nhalf of this week. Aloft...multiple waves will move through NW flow \ntriggering several rounds of showers and thunderstorms. Timing the \nprecision of these waves is proving to be difficult with a wide \nspread of solutions showing up in the guidance. Tonight...showers \nwill continue into this evening before easing up a bit in coverage \nover night and early Tuesday as an initial wave departs the area \nhowever there is considerable uncertainty with the CAMS models \nwhere some do develop more convection during the wee hours. \n\nOne thing that is becoming more clear is that instability will \nreturn along and south of the warm frontal position setting the area \nup for stronger, more organized storms. Tuesday afternoon looks to \nbe the warmer day, with ensemble probabilities eclipsing 70% for \n>1500 J/kg of CAPE along with around 40 knots of shear. One of the \nthings working against Tuesday is the lack of a definitive trigger \ncoinciding with the instability and shear parameters, but even if \nstorms occur early or after dark, severe storms would be likely due \nto the prime atmospheric setup. It is difficult to pin down any one \nlocation at this time, however, the instability axis favors our \nwestern and southern zones.\n\n\nWednesday has a stronger shortwave passing through with the dominant \nmodel cluster showing a later day/night timing if correct. More \nmoisture aloft will likely mean more clouds, less heating and \noverall CAPE development during the day. Still somewhere between 800 \nand 1500 J/kg is looking likely. Shear will be stronger, hovering \naround 50 knots and with the shortwave passing through, a trigger is \npresent to get storms going even without a whole lot of CAPE. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThe warm front will still be stalled and could be a focal point for \ntraining storms mainly on Wednesday and Wednesday night which will \nbe the primary focus for a higher potential for flooding. PWATs near \n2 inches and a strong (~40 to 50 knot) low level jet forming in the \nevening on Wednesday feeding persistent, training storms could lead \nto significant flash flooding where they line up. The current \nthinking points to a cumulative spread of QPF between 2-3\" of rain \nover the next 72 hours across CNY and a little less for NEPA. \nLocally higher amounts from persistent thunderstorm activity also \ncan't be ruled out within this setup as deep layer moisture and high \nwarm cloud depths will be present. National Water Model guidance and \nHREF ensemble guidance suggests that main stem rivers should be able \nto handle runoff from projected rainfall, however, smaller order \nstreams and tributaries are showing low-medium probability signals \nfor rapid onset flooding which will need close monitoring over the \nnext few days. \n\nWith low level moisture remaining in place Thursday with more sun, \ninstability develops again with high shear once again though the \nlack of a trigger may keep storms from developing but could have \nanother day with a hail and wet microburst threat.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3... \n\nFor Friday into the weekend, The large ridge in the central US \nretrogrades west with the trough in the northeast nudging into the \nmid Atlantic. This will help the flow become more northerly with \ncooler and drier air advecting in. Depending on how cool the 850 mb \ntemperatures are, there could be some lake effect rain showers to \ndeal with but for the most part the weekend is looking mostly dry.",
